{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Speak to the children of Israel and say, etc.</b> All of this is superfluous after it said ״וַיְדַבֵּר וְגוֹ׳ לֵאמֹר״ (and He spoke… saying). Perhaps it intends to speak in accordance with their words, which they said (Torat Kohanim): the children of Israel contract impurity through zivah, but the nations of the world do not contract impurity through zivah. Therefore He said <b>דַּבְּרוּ</b> — meaning, an expression of difficulty: that you find impurity in them more than in the gentiles; an Israelite zav impurifies bedding and seating, and a gentile zav does not impurify bedding and seating. And He said <b>וַאֲמַרְתֶּם</b> — meaning, an expression of elevation, along the lines of what they said (Deuteronomy 26:17) ״הֶאֱמַרְתָּ״ and ״הֶאֱמִירְךָ״. The intention in this is that even though He imposes upon them more impurity, this is an elevation for them, for they exist in purity, and the nations of the world do not exist in purity; and it is the sod of ״אֵין שׁוֹטֶה נִפְגָּע״ (Shabbat 13a) — ״a fool is not afflicted.״" "<b>A man, a man, when he has a discharge, etc.</b> One must know why He repeated to say <b>אִישׁ אִישׁ</b>. Perhaps it is to inform of what they said, of blessed memory, in the midrash (Tana D'vei Eliyahu, chapter 15): A man had a keri discharge that obligates immersion, and he said: ״Who sees me? It is nothing.״ Again he had a second occurrence — he said: ״Who sees me?״ A third time — he transgressed what is written: ״פַּעֲמַיִם שָׁלֹשׁ עִם גָּבֶר״ (Job 33:29) — he becomes a zav, etc., end quote. This is what He alluded to by saying <b>אִישׁ אִישׁ</b> — meaning, the first time: אִישׁ, along the lines of what they said (Deuteronomy 23:11) ״אִישׁ כִּי לֹא יִהְיֶה טָהוֹר מִקְּרֵה לָיְלָה״; and He said a second time: אִישׁ, to allude to the second occurrence — if he repented, well and good; if not, he becomes a complete zav and immerses for severe impurity." "<b>His discharge — it is impure.</b> Our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded in Torat Kohanim along these lines: ״זוֹבוֹ הוּא טָמֵא״ — this teaches about the discharge itself that it is impure. But that is a matter of logical inference (kal va-chomer): the zav, who himself caused impurity, is impure — the discharge, which causes impurity, all the more so…; the scapegoat proves it, etc., for it is pure yet impurifies — therefore Scripture says ״זוֹבוֹ הוּא טָמֵא״. And they further expounded along these lines: ״הוּא טָמֵא״ — but blood that exits from the male organ is not impure. Meaning, I would have reasoned by kal va-chomer from saliva: now if saliva, which exits from a place of purity, is impure, then blood that exits from a place of impurity — is it not a logical inference, etc.? Scripture says ״הוּא״ to exclude it. And in the manner that the Tanna there reasoned when he wished to require an inclusion for urine, which impurifies, and reasoned a kal va-chomer from saliva and then refuted it from exiting blood — Scripture says ״וְזֹאת״ to include urine. And in the chapter Dam Nidah (Niddah 56a) they raised the difficulty: And what did you see to include urine and to exclude exiting blood? And Rabbi Shimon said: I include urine for impurity, for it resembles saliva, which becomes agglutinated and exits and then returns and is absorbed — unlike blood, end quote." "And it is difficult: if so, Scripture needed to write only the inclusion of ״וְזֹאת״, and I sit and examine why this inclusion is necessary — for both urine and exiting blood would equally be derived by kal va-chomer from saliva. And by force of this I would conclude that one should not reason a kal va-chomer, and we would say that the verse comes to refute the kal va-chomer that would obligate you to include both urine and exiting blood, yet it included only one of them. And to know which of them is the one included, one would reason from that which resembles saliva, which becomes agglutinated, etc., according to the words of Rabbi Shimon — and there would have been no need to write the exclusion ״הוּא.״ And one cannot dismiss this by saying: then I would say that the inclusion of ״וְזֹאת״ came for exiting blood, even though it does not become agglutinated, etc., whereas urine would come by kal va-chomer — this is an error, for when we come to reason by kal va-chomer from saliva, which does not exit from a place of impurity, we reason urine and exiting blood together in the kal va-chomer, for they are equal in the stringent respect, that they exited from a place of impurity, and no inclusion is needed for blood unless you say that the verse came to refute the kal va-chomer. And you will find that the Tanna, too, who derived the exclusion of blood from the exclusion ״הוּא,״ when he came to derive urine from the kal va-chomer of saliva, rejected it with ״the exiting blood will prove it״ — that it is not derived by kal va-chomer — and did not reject this challenge with ״what about exiting blood, which does not become agglutinated,״ etc. From this point on, if there were only the inclusion of ״וְזֹאת,״ you would be forced by your own reasoning to say that the Torah restrained you from reasoning this kal va-chomer so that you would not derive everything, and I know that that which comes to declare pure is the exiting blood." "And from this force I have constructed an opening to explain that the verse intended, by the extra word ״הוּא,״ to say: it is not the body of the zav alone that impurifies its bearer, but rather it is a sign that HaShem makes in the impure person corresponding to his evil deeds. And HaShem made known that a man in whom this sign appears — it is a sign to him that he is an impure man. And the word ״הוּא״ that the verse said refers back to the man — that he is impure. And therefore HaShem decrees the law of the man himself, that he himself is a father of impurity, to impurify persons and utensils. And even though the Tanna expounded it to exclude blood, that was before the exposition of ״וְזֹאת״; and after the exposition of ״וְזֹאת״ we return to derive and to expound anew. And many times Torat Kohanim expounds in this manner, and here it left an opening for those who expound Torah."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {2 "Or HaChaim's opening attribution ״רז״ל״ likely refers to the Tanna of Torat Kohanim rather than to Chazal in general, given that he proceeds to present the Tanna's specific derivation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:57:33.683332406Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}